# Self-hosted runner operations

This repository's CI and release workflows run only on the dedicated
`SpecsAgentRunner` WSL2 distribution. The GitHub Actions runner is named
`specs-agent-wsl-01`; its systemd unit is
`actions.runner.bassemZohdy-specs-agent-platform.specs-agent-wsl-01.service`.
Its GitHub runner labels are
`self-hosted`, `Linux`, `X64`, and `specs-agent` (workflow label matching is
case-insensitive). GitHub-hosted fallback is intentionally not configured.

## Security boundary

Treat this as a private, trusted-code runner: repository collaborators who can
change workflow code can execute commands in the runner environment. The
isolated WSL distribution contains its own Docker Engine, so Docker access is
effectively privileged inside that distribution. Do not run workflows from
untrusted forks, and keep repository write/admin access tightly controlled.

`SpecsAgentRunner` disables Windows automount and interop. This prevents jobs
from automatically seeing Windows drives or launching Windows executables; do
not weaken those settings merely for a workflow convenience.

## Health and routine checks

From Windows PowerShell, confirm the service and runner process are healthy.
The runner account has no `sudo`; use WSL's root user for systemd operations:

```powershell
wsl.exe -d SpecsAgentRunner -u root --exec systemctl is-active actions.runner.bassemZohdy-specs-agent-platform.specs-agent-wsl-01.service
wsl.exe -d SpecsAgentRunner -u root --exec systemctl status actions.runner.bassemZohdy-specs-agent-platform.specs-agent-wsl-01.service --no-pager
```

From an authenticated GitHub CLI session, confirm GitHub sees the expected
online runner and labels:

```bash
gh api repos/OWNER/REPOSITORY/actions/runners \
  --jq '.runners[] | select(.name == "specs-agent-wsl-01") | {status, busy, labels: [.labels[].name]}'
```

The runner is offline while Windows sleeps or the user logs off, because WSL2
stops with that user session. A current-user **HKCU Run** keepalive starts WSL
and the service after sign-in; it is deliberately not a Scheduled Task because
creating one requires administrative rights in this setup. After Windows or
WSL recovery, start the distribution, then verify the systemd service and the
GitHub API result above before relying on queued jobs.

Docker data lives in the isolated distribution. Periodically inspect usage with
`docker system df`; prune only data no longer needed by active workloads, for
example `docker image prune` or `docker builder prune` after reviewing their
targets. Avoid broad `docker system prune --volumes` on this persistent runner.

## Startup, recovery, and rollback

For normal startup or recovery, sign in to the Windows account, allow the HKCU
Run keepalive to launch WSL, then run the health checks. If the service is not
active, inspect and restart it from Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
wsl.exe -d SpecsAgentRunner -u root --exec journalctl -u actions.runner.bassemZohdy-specs-agent-platform.specs-agent-wsl-01.service --no-pager
wsl.exe -d SpecsAgentRunner -u root --exec systemctl restart actions.runner.bassemZohdy-specs-agent-platform.specs-agent-wsl-01.service
```

Do not start a second interactive runner process while the service owns the
registration.

To remove the runner safely, first disable or update repository workflows so no
jobs target `specs-agent`, then wait for or cancel active jobs. Stop and disable
the systemd service from Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
wsl.exe -d SpecsAgentRunner -u root --exec systemctl disable --now actions.runner.bassemZohdy-specs-agent-platform.specs-agent-wsl-01.service
```

Then remove the runner registration from GitHub, remove the HKCU Run keepalive
entry, and only then unregister the WSL distribution if it is no longer needed.
This order avoids stranded jobs and prevents an old runner credential from
reconnecting.
